58,3,,status,Midori,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
59,3,,status,Benjamin,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
60,3,1,talk,May,,"Look at these! Plan A is basically the contactless pilot on steroids, and Plan D mirrors that late-evening success. I'm leaning toward A or D—let's actually use what we know works!","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"Plan D is certainly the most honest about the human cost, as it explicitly mentions adjusting rosters. However, I wonder if ""adjusted"" is a euphemism for ""further stretched."" @Jonathan, does the ledger allow for new hires?","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Midori,,"Plan B seems most promising to me. Since mid-city runs are taking longer and passengers are losing time, dedicated lanes and better boarding could actually fix the bottlenecks where time is being lost.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"I am a bit concerned about Plan C. While improving access is wonderful, those older stations are the pre-war shells where we found those hidden voids. I worry it might lead to many unexpected delays.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"@Benjamin, the ledger is precarious. We have ring-fenced funds from a lapsed grant, but operating subsidies are being trimmed. I worry Plan A might just replace one set of contractor fees with another.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,May,,"@Jonathan, you're always worrying about the fees! But Plan A is a proven concept. If we want to stop the bleeding, we should go with something that actually works instead of guessing with Plan B or C.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"If we prioritize ""proven concepts"" over the people who must implement them, we are merely polishing the brass on a sinking ship. Plan D is the only one that acknowledges the roster, though I remain skeptical.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Midori,,"@Benjamin, I understand your skepticism, but Plan B targets the actual delays I found in the mid-city runs. If we don't fix the travel time, we're just managing a slow system more efficiently.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"I understand your point, @Midori. However, I am also thinking about Plan A. Since the ferry and rail offices have not agreed on data terms, a single platform might be very difficult to implement smoothly.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"I agree with @Shizue. Plan A risks creating a new, expensive contractual knot. Plan B seems more grounded in physical reality, though we must ensure the paving costs don't spiral further.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,May,,"@Jonathan, @Shizue, you're both so cautious you're practically standing still! Plan A is a win we've already had. Why are we acting like it's a gamble? Let's be bold for once and actually implement it!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Jonathan"",""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"Boldness is a virtue, May, provided it isn't blind. Plan A ignores the staffing crisis entirely. I find Plan D's focus on rosters intriguing, though Plan B's physical focus is certainly more grounded than a digital fantasy.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Midori,,"I appreciate @Benjamin's openness to Plan B. If we want to stop passengers from losing time, we have to address the mid-city surface delays. It's a practical solution that targets the actual bottleneck.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Shizue,,It is very heartening to see us finding common ground. I feel Plan B is a gentle and practical choice; it avoids the hidden dangers of the old shells and the disagreements over data.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,I agree with @Shizue and @Midori. Plan B addresses the mid-city delays without the contractual risks of Plan A or the structural dangers of Plan C. It seems the most sustainable and honest path forward.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shizue"",""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,May,,"Fine, you've all ganged up on me! I still think we're ignoring the easy wins, but I can't fight the whole room. If you're all so sure about the buses, I'll go with Plan B.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"It appears the consensus has shifted toward the pragmatic. While I still harbor concerns about who will actually drive these buses, Plan B is far less delusional than the alternatives. I shall cast my vote for Plan B.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Midori,,I'm so glad we could reach an agreement. Plan B is the most effective way to reduce the actual time passengers spend in transit. I'm happy to vote for Plan B.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Shizue,,It has been such a lovely discussion. I am glad we could all listen to one another and find a path that feels safe and practical. I will also vote for Plan B.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,I am glad we have found a consensus. Plan B offers a tangible improvement to the network while avoiding the long-term financial traps of complex contracts. I will also vote for Plan B.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
